VIM4 USB-PD boot snapshot (v1)
============================

The FUSB302 driver records negotiation events without extra register accesses
or delays. ft_board_setup() copies
that snapshot into the final Linux FDT after /chosen creation and before FDT
packing. Both bootm and booti reach image_setup_linux() / image_setup_libfdt();
the selected kernel's loaded DTB is used, not U-Boot's control DTB. kvim4 has
CONFIG_OF_BOARD_SETUP=y. The optional handoff failing logs an error but does not
abort the boot. Runtime tests on both kernels remain required before deployment.

No existing Khadas PD handoff property was found in this tree. The private
/chosen/khadas,usb-pd-boot property is exactly 16 big-endian u32 cells (64 bytes).
It describes this firmware's finite startup attempt, not the live Linux contract.

Cell   Meaning
0      Format version, 1
1      Flags: bit 0 PS_RDY observed; 1 received hard reset; 2 sent hard reset;
       3 Request transmission failed / Reject / Wait; 4 loop expired;
       5 I2C/probe error; 6 sink attached; 7 no confirmed PD at exit (Type-C fallback)
2      Last evaluated Source_Capabilities header (raw 16 bits)
3      Last attempted Request header (raw 16 bits)
4      Last attempted RDO (raw); selected position is bits 30:28
5      Source PDO count, 0..7
6      Observed sink CC BC_LVL: 0 unknown/open, 1 USB default, 2 1.5A, 3 3A
7..8   Driver policy target in mV / mA, NOT the actual RDO operating values
9..15  Raw Source_Capabilities PDOs, unused cells zero

PS_RDY alone is not proof that all fields are valid: consumers must validate
version, size, headers, selected PDO, RDO limits and I/O-error/fallback flags.
Hard reset clears PS_RDY; a later successful attempt may set it again. Error
flags are sticky for this initialization. Rp is the level already observed at
attachment, not a new current measurement; USB-default does not imply 3 A or a
fixed numerical current. Fallback means no confirmed PD contract at exit, not
proof of a successful post-reset Type-C power transition.

Decode PDO types using the USB PD definitions (Fixed=0, Battery=1, Variable=2,
APDO=3; APDO subtype 0=PPS). Source and Request headers each carry revision in
bits 7:6 (0=1.0, 1=2.0, 2=3.0). These do not identify PD 3.1 minor revisions.
Fixed voltage comes from its PDO; operating current comes from RDO bits 19:10
(10 mA). Battery uses power, not current; Variable has a voltage range, not a
requested voltage; PPS voltage/current come from its RDO. Preserve unknown APDOs
as raw values. Never substitute policy values or source ratings for Request.

The KVM policy selects the first nonzero-current Fixed 5V PDO and caps both
operating and maximum current at 3A. It sets USB Communications Capable and
No USB Suspend, giving RDO 0x1304b12c for PDO1 at 5V/3A. The ceiling is not a
minimum power requirement, so a 1.5A offer requests 1.5A without Capability
Mismatch. With no usable 5V Fixed PDO, the existing wait-caps/timeout path is
used without sending a Request. The former charge-IC 12V/6A selection policy
is removed for every board using this driver: high-voltage charging and
Battery/Variable PDO requests are no longer supported by this KVM branch.
This does not change receive timing, add Get_Source_Cap or Hard_Reset, or fix
the existing IRQ ordering, Soft_Reset and post-boot protocol service gaps.

Older firmware may record 0x1401912c (1A operating, 3A maximum) for a 5V/3A
offer. The v1 format is unchanged; consumers must still decode the raw RDO.

Run python3 tools/test-vim4-pd-handoff.py for host-only regression with the actual
driver functions, mocked controller I/O and this tree's libfdt. It tests the
Request bytes, PS_RDY gate, receive-buffer reuse, hard reset and both DT shapes.
Pass --linux-pd-source /path/to/vim4-kvm/tools/usb-pd-init.c to compare the
actual Linux request_5v implementation for every nonzero 10mA current value.
Linux requires PDO1 to be Fixed 5V; this firmware can select a later Fixed 5V
PDO and preserves that object's position in the RDO.
